#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# q_pochhammer

#% text
# `q_pochhammer(a, q, n)` gives the q-Pochhammer symbol `(a; q)_n`, the product of `1 - a*q^k` for `k` from 0 to `n - 1`.

#% text
# `q_pochhammer(a, q)` gives the infinite product `(a; q)_inf`, for `abs(q) < 1`.

#% text
# `q_pochhammer(q)` gives `(q; q)_inf`, Euler's function.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 17.2.1; `(a; q)_0` is 1. A finite product with symbolic arguments is expanded into factors.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values for the infinite product.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
q_pochhammer(a, q, 3)
#% output
#= (1 - a)*(1 - a*q)*(1 - a*q^2)

#% cell
q_pochhammer(0.5, 0.5, 4)
#% output
#= 0.3076171875

#% cell
q_pochhammer(0.5, 0.3)
#% output
#= 0.3980822043018777

#% cell
q_pochhammer(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.2887880950866024

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Euler's function generates partitions: `1/(q; q)_inf` has the partition numbers as coefficients, so at a small `q` the leading terms show:

#% cell
1/q_pochhammer(0.01)
#% output
#= 1.0102030507111521

#% cell
sum(partitions_p(k)*0.01^k, [k, 0, 10])
#% output
#= 1.0102030507111524

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# As `q` tends to 1, `(q^a; q)_n/(1 - q)^n` tends to the Pochhammer symbol `(a)_n`:

#% cell
q_pochhammer(0.9999^3, 0.9999, 4)/(1 - 0.9999)^4
#% output
#= 359.7480878801689

#% cell
pochhammer(3, 4)
#% output
#= 360

